{
  "configuration_id": "config_031",
  "reason": "The strongest RNA-only multiprofile baseline (scvi_n200, avgpool) achieves ~65.0% accuracy, but adding the image modality via concat fusion (config_030) actually *decreased* performance to ~63.2%. The critical uncertainty is whether this ~1.8-point drop is caused by the concat fusion mechanism (which treats both modalities equally and cannot down-weight a weak signal) or by the image content itself being non-complementary. Config_031 changes only the fusion from concat to weighted, keeping all other settings identical to config_030 (scvi_n200, ResNet50, multiprofile, avgpool). This single-variable comparison isolates the fusion strategy: if weighted fusion recovers to ≥65%, it indicates the image carries complementary signal that concat fails to exploit; if it remains below 65%, it strongly suggests the ResNet50-encoded image is fundamentally non-complementary under this setup, saving budget from further image-encoder or aggregation variants. This is the most information-dense next experiment because it directly addresses the key open question (can multimodal beat RNA-only?) with minimal confounding, and the answer will determine whether subsequent budget should go toward stronger image encoders or toward RNA-only encoder exploration (e.g., scgpt)."
}

